WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.467
Behavior Lab is my behavioral event pipeline project. A fictional Canadian corporate events

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:12.933
company, Northline Events, provides the business context. The interactive application connects customer actions

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:19.400
to inspectable data and client insights.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:26.700
Corporate event activity spans browsing, ticket purchases, meeting requests and attendance. I built

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:34.000
one application that captures these actions and preserves the evidence behind each profile.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.633
The playground contains a mobile event application. Visitors can explore events, place demo

00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:45.267
ticket and merchandise orders, book a consultation, save event briefs, request proposals and

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:50.900
simulate ticket entry.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:56.633
Each action reaches the Flask API. Validation and identity checks precede a transactional

00:00:56.633 --> 00:01:02.367
SQLite write. An outbox publisher sends records to Kafka topics. The consumer commits

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:08.100
offsets after storing the processed result.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:13.833
Bronze stores raw event payloads. Silver records the effective contribution of each action.

00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:19.567
Gold holds the visitor profile. Event identities support duplicate handling, while capture order

00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:25.300
supports consistent reconstruction.

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:31.100
Insights aggregate preferences and commercial activity. Meeting bookings and proposal requests guide follow-up.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:36.900
Ticket orders, merchandise sales and check-ins reconcile in organizer reports. Synthetic activity is

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:42.700
labeled and filterable.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.850
Seventeen pipeline tests passed. API checks reconciled three hundred and thirty Canadian dollars

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:55.000
in demo purchases. Browser checks traced a ticket purchase and entry into reports.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.150
GitHub Actions also passed a real Kafka smoke test, covering broker acknowledgment, consumer

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:07.300
materialization and duplicate retries. Public backend hosting remains pending.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:13.700
The source is published on GitHub under Project Behavior Lab. The repository includes

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:20.100
Docker configuration, a real broker smoke test and GitHub Actions. This project demonstrates

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:26.500
event modeling, delivery reliability, database processing and explainable business insights.
